    "
  >
    Spotlight
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    BRICS rift widens
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> India’s FM Subrahmanyam Jaishankar urged BRICS nations to protect “safe, unimpeded maritime flows” through international waters, especially the Strait of Hormuz, as foreign ministers met in New Delhi. The meeting comes amid <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-tensions-oil-shocks-dominate-brics-talks-in-india/a-77158355" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">deep divisions inside the expanded BRICS bloc</a>, particularly over the US-Israel war with Iran and tensions between Iran and the UAE. Talks are expected to focus on energy security, trade in local currencies, sanctions, and keeping critical oil and gas routes open.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why?</strong> The BRICS foreign ministers’ meeting matters because it tests whether the expanded bloc can come together as a global force during a major geopolitical crisis. With Iran, the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Russia, China, India, Brazil, South Africa, Egypt, Ethiopia, and Indonesia under one umbrella, the group epresents competing interests as much as shared goals. The meeting provides a possibility for stability.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Iran’s appeal:</strong> FM Abbas Araghchi urged BRICS members to clearly and unanimously condemn the US and Israel for attacks on Iran. He framed the strikes as illegal aggression and a violation of international law, pushing the bloc to show solidarity with Tehran. But the demand exposed BRICS’ internal divisions, especially because members such as the UAE and Saudi Arabia are unlikely to back strong anti-Israel or anti-US language.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
