<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px" border-radius="5px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Cuba goes dark
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Cuba’s energy crisis has deepened after Energy Minister Vicente de la O Levy said the country has <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd7pyrj0vx7o" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">completely run out of diesel and fuel oil</a>. Minimal gas from domestic wells remains, leaving the national grid in a “critical” state. Parts of Havana are facing blackouts lasting up to 20-22 hours, while hospitals, schools, government offices, and tourism have been disrupted.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why? </strong>Cuba’s fuel crisis has worsened because the island depends heavily on imported oil, especially from allies such as Venezuela and Mexico. Those goods have fallen after US pressure and tariff threats against countries sending fuel to Cuba. Without enough diesel and fuel oil, power plants cannot run normally, pushing the electricity grid into severe shortage. Cuba’s aging energy infrastructure, weak economy, and minimal foreign currency have made the crisis harder to manage.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>US response: </strong>The US says Cuba’s government is responsible for worsening hardship by refusing outside support and blocking reform. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Havana rejected a $100M humanitarian aid, though Cuba denied this. The State Department repeated the proposal, saying aid could be delivered through the Catholic Church and trusted humanitarian groups. Washington also linked assistance to “meaningful reforms” in Cuba’s communist system.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
