        <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
<h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  Ramaphosa under fire
</h2></mj-text><mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="0px 12px 20px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000"><p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What happened? </strong>South African President Cyril Ramaphosa is facing renewed pressure over the Phala Phala scandal, with Parliament set to <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-south-africas-president-ramaphosa-avoid-impeachment-again/a-77132788" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">restart an impeachment process</a> against him. Ramaphosa has said he will not resign and plans to challenge the proceedings through judicial review. A Constitutional Court ruling has revived the issue, forcing Parliament to set up a Section 89 committee.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Will he be impeached? </strong>South Africa’s Parliament can oust a president under Section 89 of the Constitution if there is a violation of the Constitution, misconduct, or inability to perform duties. Ramaphosa can be removed if two-thirds of MPs vote to impeach him. That is a high threshold, especially because the political landscape is fragmented and his coalition partner, the Democratic Alliance, has not clearly backed impeachment.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Background: </strong>The Phala Phala case began after assertions that a large amount of currency was stolen from Ramaphosa’s Phala Phala Wildlife farm in 2020. The scandal became public in June 2022 when former spy chief Arthur Fraser alleged that nearly $4M had been stolen and that the matter was handled through an unlawful investigation. Ramaphosa said the currency was $580,000 from the trade of buffalo to a Sudanese businessman.</p></mj-text>
        <!-- </mj-text> -->
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
